# text
# c1: clock-holdover.toml
seg-45da99dccd40 = A recorded session with the kshana-mcp server (Model Context Protocol, engine v{ $version }): the assistant lists the { "49" } tools, validates <c1/> (valid: detected scenario kind `clock`) and runs it: scenario 5ba83a232b94 | quantum holdover 6600s p95 1.20e-4ns integrity { "1.000" } security n/a (no attack) | classical holdover 2610s p95 19.7ns integrity { "1.000" } security n/a (no attack).

# text
# c1: animate_scenario
seg-102015d6ebd2 = <c1/>: Run a Kshana scenario and return its time series as an animation.

# text
# c1: assess_receiver_log
seg-0247e08ba09d = <c1/>: Assess a real GNSS receiver log for trust: read a u-blox UBX, RINEX { "3" } (optionally with broadcast navigation, which adds the engine's own fix, RAIM and a clock-aided monitor), Android GnssLogger or NMEA log, run the trust monitors (carrier-to-noise density drop, AGC, jamming indicator, loss of lock, position jump, RAIM, clock) against a calibration baseline, and return when and why the receiver stopped being trustworthy.

# text
# c1: assess_vessel_log
seg-155a62e8bc94 = <c1/>: Assess a vessel's NMEA { "0183" } log as a batch run (`kshana receiver-trust` with `[platform] kind = "vessel"`): the score model, the monitors that ran and every epoch's 0-100 score with its deductions, its satellite geometry (good, degraded, unknown) with a `label` (good, fair, poor) on every epoch, geometry deducting only when poor, and a class on every deduction (geometry, environment, inconsistency), with `geometry_model` (thresholds, the cap and floor in force, and provenance) and `geometry_summary`.

# text
# c1: assess_vessel_stream
seg-8ff4e06de726 = <c1/>: Score a bounded excerpt of a vessel's NMEA { "0183" } stream the way `kshana receiver-trust live` scores it, and return per-epoch trust state (calibrating, nominal, degraded, untrusted) and 0-100 score with the monitors that deducted (live JSON-lines schema { "1.3" }: each epoch carries `position`, an `advisory` statement that the output is advisory, and its satellite `geometry` (good, degraded with reasons such as few-satellites or high-pdop, or unknown when the excerpt has no GSV or GSA) with a `label` (good, fair, poor; geometry deducts only when poor); every deduction carries a `class`: geometry (the sky, not a finding that no attack occurred), environment or inconsistency (attack-like)), the summary (counts by state, geometry and class, lowest and final score, when the first untrusted epoch came) and the last `$PKSHT` sentence.

# text
# c1: attach_evidence_timestamp
seg-7de08fd247e0 = <c1/>: Bind an RFC { "3161" } timestamp token to an evidence pack (`kshana evidence attach-timestamp`), in memory: `files` is a pack as `create_evidence_pack` returns it, `token_base64` the bytes of the `.tsr` file from a timestamp authority.

# text
# c1: build_interference_map
seg-dc1953969ec4 = <c1/>: Build a GNSS interference map from openly licensed aircraft (ADS-B NIC/NACp) or ship (AIS) position reports given as CSV text (`kshana interference-map adsb|ais`).

# text
# c1: compliance_mapping
seg-7a7a1e7a3f0d = <c1/>: The static public-framework mapping (`kshana compliance-report --mapping` / `--sources`) as Markdown: one table per framework with each row's paraphrased ask, the Kshana outputs that support evidence for it and the gap the outputs do not close; or, with `sources` true, the source documents the tables cite with versions and URLs.

# text
# c1: compliance_report
seg-21caa50ec277 = <c1/>: Fill the public-framework mapping from Kshana result documents (`kshana compliance-report`): which rows of six resilience frameworks and standards (the US DHS Resilient PNT framework v2.0, IMO guidance for ships, EASA guidance for aviation, NIS2 Article { "21" }, EN { "16803" }, NIST IR 8323r1) the runs given support evidence for, which they do not, and the gap each row keeps.

# text
# c1: create_evidence_pack
seg-e9202a8c1176 = <c1/>: Create a signed evidence pack for a window of a vessel's NMEA { "0183" } log (`kshana receiver-trust evidence`): the raw log bytes for the window, the configuration with every threshold, the per-epoch results and reasons, a self-contained summary page, and a manifest listing every file's SHA-256 with the full log's hash and a hash chain, signed with Ed25519.

# text
# c1: create_round_trip_pack
seg-f09af0fa8fcd = <c1/>: Create the signed laboratory round-trip pack (`kshana evidence round-trip`, docs/spec/TEST-CONDITIONS.md): links by hash the scenario that produced the motion, the laboratory's `kshana.test-conditions/1` document, the receiver log and its score against the stated events, in a signed evidence pack whose `config.json` carries a `round_trip` block.

# text
# c1: export_interop
seg-404efdf9e8d2 = <c1/>: Export a Kshana scenario in one interoperability format: `czml` (Cesium Language, for CesiumJS), `kml` (Keyhole Markup Language, for Google Earth and geographic information system tools), `geojson` (RFC { "7946" }), `stk` (Systems Tool Kit ephemeris `.e`, one file per satellite) or `sigmf` (Signal Metadata Format recording of a `spectrum` scenario's synthesised samples).

# text
# c1: export_oem
seg-91fc7df72b42 = <c1/>: Export an `orbit` scenario's propagated constellation as CCSDS OEM { "2.0" } ephemeris text — the inertial (TEME) state time series carrying position AND velocity, which flight-dynamics tools (GMAT / Orekit / STK) read.

# text
# c1: export_omm
seg-e7086c7b5491 = <c1/>: Export an `orbit` scenario's mean elements as a CCSDS 502.0-B-2 OMM (Orbit Mean-Elements Message) catalogue — one OMM per satellite.

# text
# c1: export_sp3
seg-5689ddc45bb8 = <c1/>: Export an `orbit` scenario's propagated constellation as SP3-c precise-ephemeris text (the standard GNSS post-processing format).

# text
# c1: export_table_csv
seg-73b7eb38abe9 = <c1/>: Run a Kshana scenario and return its reproducibility table as CSV text — the byte-stable table the CLI writes as `<scenario>.table.csv` and the papers cite.

# text
# c1: export_test_bench
seg-afec41e44d7a = <c1/>: Export a scenario's vehicle motion and events for a laboratory GNSS simulator (`kshana bench-export`, docs/TEST-BENCH.md): a motion CSV and its JSON description (Earth-fixed and geodetic position, velocity, attitude, UTC), NMEA { "0183" } GGA/RMC sentences, a waypoint text when the sample grid is millisecond-regular, and the scenario's events as CSV and as `[[events]]` TOML for a `receiver-trust` scenario.

# text
# c1: fit_imu_log
seg-cffb261ea30e = <c1/>: Fit the sensor parameters of the `hybrid` and `fusion` scenarios from a stationary IMU log (`kshana imu-fit`, docs/MACHINE-INPUTS.md).

# text
# c1: generate_training_nmea
seg-c31f25888d91 = <c1/>: Generate synthetic bridge NMEA { "0183" } for crew training from a `nmea-scenario` TOML (`kshana nmea-scenario`): a vessel track and a timeline of scripted events (jamming, position drag-off, time spoof, replay delay, with recovery).

# text
# c1: get_example_scenario
seg-b1ed1e518609 = <c1/>: Return the TOML text of one bundled reference scenario, byte for byte the file the repository ships under `scenarios/`.

# text
# c1: import_route
seg-aafdb73abfac = <c1/>: Write a GeoJSON route into a Kshana scenario that flies a waypoint track, and return the new scenario TOML (run it with run_scenario).

# text
# c1: iq_acquire
seg-21086f42f34d = <c1/>: FFT acquisition of one or more PRNs over an IQ recording in the work directory (`kshana iq acquire`).

# text
# c1: iq_campaign
seg-4091e4073457 = <c1/>: Run a lab-replay campaign held in the work directory (`kshana iq campaign`): recordings (each described by a `kshana.test-conditions/1` file stating the lab's known truth) x front-end chains x loop designs, each cell tracked and scored against the stated conditions (time to loss of lock, re-acquisition time, C/N0 degradation against the stated J/S beside an analytic reference labelled MODELLED, false-lock rate, PLL/DLL jitter, availability).

# text
# c1: iq_campaign_status
seg-df54bfe48786 = <c1/>: Report a lab-replay campaign's progress from its output folder in the work directory, reading files only: cells done and pending, the recordings, front ends and designs in the plan, and the `digest` once every cell is done.

# text
# c1: iq_frontend
seg-ddc737c9ba9a = <c1/>: Apply receiver front-end and interference-mitigation DSP to an IQ recording in the work directory and write the result as a new raw recording with a `<out>.json` sidecar (`kshana iq frontend`).

# text
# c1: iq_info
seg-0f9fef6134e1 = <c1/>: Describe one IQ recording in the work directory without processing it: kind (raw with sidecar, SigMF, SigMF collection), sample format, sample rate, centre frequency, total samples, duration, data files and their size, capture boundaries and annotation count, as JSON (the same row `kshana iq info` prints).

# text
# c1: iq_labfit
seg-d416e59af6b1 = <c1/>: Fit the tracking-loop loss-of-lock model to lab runs (`kshana iq labfit`): an `iq-labfit` scenario TOML names one or more runs, each a receiver log plus its stated test conditions; the engine loads each log, fits the loop and empirical models, bootstraps the uncertainty and computes the hold-out error.

# text
# c1: iq_monitor
seg-a409849c2544 = <c1/>: Run the interference and spoofing detection monitors over an IQ recording in the work directory (`kshana iq monitor`): the wideband `power` and `spectral` monitors, and, given `signal` + `prns`, the per-satellite C/N0 and signal-quality monitors, each against a baseline learned from the first `baseline_s` seconds (or from a settings file).

# text
# c1: iq_scene
seg-442716c37495 = <c1/>: Generate a multi-satellite GNSS IQ scene into a file in the work directory (`kshana iq scene`).

# text
# c1: iq_signals
seg-d965c4c42205 = <c1/>: The GNSS IQ layer's set-up, as JSON: `signals` (the signal names iq_scene, iq_acquire and iq_track accept), `enabled` (whether the IQ file tools are on), `work_dir` (the only folder they read and write; every path argument is relative to it), `max_samples` (the most complex samples one call may generate or process) and, when off, `reason`.

# text
# c1: iq_sweep
seg-0709a11ca4ed = <c1/>: Replay one IQ recording in the work directory across several tracking-loop designs and report each design's steady-state result (`kshana iq sweep`): the designs are every design of a `kshana.loop-design/1` file (`design`), or the product of the `pll_bws_hz`, `dll_bws_hz`, `spacings_chips` and `coherents` lists on the built-in design.

# text
# c1: iq_test_conditions
seg-155a371604fb = <c1/>: Validate a lab test-condition file (`kshana iq conditions`): a `kshana.test-conditions/1` file, TOML or JSON text, stating the lab's known truth for a recording (the satellites expected, the settle time, the stated jamming or interference schedule).

# text
# c1: iq_track
seg-e485776388d7 = <c1/>: Acquire then track one or more PRNs over an IQ recording in the work directory (`kshana iq track`): acquisition initialises each channel, then the DLL/PLL (optionally FLL-assisted) loop bank replays the recording once.

# text
# c1: jammertest_import
seg-604b653903df = <c1/>: Read the published Jammertest test catalogue and transmission plan (NPRA/jammertest-plan, MIT) as scheduled truth (`kshana jammertest import`): the slots of one test area become receiver-trust events with the catalogue id and year in each label.

# text
# c1: jammertest_score
seg-32761d35a4f0 = <c1/>: Score a receiver log against the Jammertest schedule (`kshana jammertest score`): per scheduled test the detection latency (first alarm minus planned start), whether it was detected within the tolerance, late or missed, and the time to recover (an alarm-free stretch of `recover_hold_s`), plus the false alarms outside every slot and its guard.

# text
# c1: list_example_scenarios
seg-c41dc865f6f0 = <c1/>: List the bundled reference scenarios as JSON: `count`, and `scenarios`, an array of `name`, `kind` and `about` (the first sentence of the file's own header comment).

# text
# c1: list_export_formats
seg-8744e56b9d92 = <c1/>: Report which interoperability export formats apply to a Kshana scenario, without running it: a JSON array with one entry per format (`czml`, `kml`, `geojson`, `stk`, `sigmf`) giving `applies`, the `reason` when it does not, and `spec_url`, the published specification the writer follows.

# text
# c1: list_scenario_kinds
seg-a16109ccf9e7 = <c1/>: List every built-in Kshana scenario kind with its description and required/optional TOML fields, as a JSON array.

# text
# c1: list_standards_packs
seg-299e14da6de8 = <c1/>: List the standards packs (`kshana standards-pack list`): scored scenario sets for the DHS Resilient PNT framework test profiles, IMO MSC.1/Circ.1575 and ICAO Doc { "9849" } Appendix F, each with the bars it states before the run and the compliance rows it supports evidence for.

# text
# c1: lunanet_afs_check
seg-1097125b8282 = <c1/>: Check the LunaNet Augmented Forward Signal (AFS) against the published specification, pinned to LNIS Version { "5" } Applicable Document { "1" } Volume A (LSIS-AFS V1.0, { "29" } January { "2025" }) (`kshana lunanet-afs-check`, docs/LUNANET-AFS.md).

# text
# c1: render_leaderboard
seg-9a7ca52ec440 = <c1/>: Render kshana-bench result files (`kshana bench leaderboard`) as the static leaderboard page, HTML text in the reply: `results` is a list of `kshana-bench-result/1` JSON texts (at most { "256" }, { "4" } MiB each), each validated first; a file scored under another major spec version is refused because results of different major versions are never put in one table.

# text
# c1: report_scenario
seg-4b61dd090d87 = <c1/>: Run a Kshana scenario and return its report: an executive summary, the inputs, every result figure with its unit and its VALIDATED or MODELLED label, the events, the capability rows the run exercised, and a reproducibility record (engine version, scenario and result SHA-256 digests, seed, the command that reproduces it).

# text
# c1: route_exposure
seg-02b4cd768b39 = <c1/>: Summarise how much of a route runs through degraded cells of one or more interference maps (`kshana route-exposure`): for each map (one per source per UTC day, the GeoJSON `build_interference_map` returns), the route length and the shares of its length in degraded cells, in cells that were not degraded, in cells with too little sample to assess, and in cells not observed.

# text
# c1: run_scenario
seg-00f57717440f = <c1/>: Run a Kshana PNT-resilience scenario from a TOML definition and return its figures of merit.

# text
# c1: run_standards_pack
seg-de0623ad4fa4 = <c1/>: Run one standards pack (`kshana standards-pack run <id>`): every scenario in it, scored against bars stated before the run.

# text
# c1: score_benchmark
seg-ac7502865a0b = <c1/>: Score a kshana-bench submission (`kshana bench score`, bench/SPEC.md, docs/BENCH.md): a predictions file (`kshana-bench-predictions/1`) against a truth file (`kshana-bench-truth/1`), both JSON text, at most { "4" } MiB each, and return the result file (`kshana-bench-result/1`) with the spec version, task (a detection on receiver logs, b detection on IQ, c timing holdover prediction, d out-of-distribution optimism gap), split, the SHA-256 of both inputs and the task's metrics (AUC, detection probability at a false-alarm cap, RMSE per horizon, or the optimism gap).

# text
# c1: sky_geometry
seg-961963f408a6 = <c1/>: Sky geometry under an azimuth-dependent skyline (a street canyon, a harbour, a fjord, a container terminal): from a site, a time and constellation presets, two-line element sets (`tle`, with a UTC `epoch`) or satellites given as azimuth/elevation or ECEF, which satellites are visible and which are hidden by the skyline, GDOP/PDOP/HDOP/VDOP/TDOP from the gnss_lib_py-checked DOP kernel, the east-north-up cofactor and covariance with one receiver clock per system (or one in common), and the horizontal error ellipse at one sigma and at a stated confidence for a stated UERE.

# text
# c1: trust_telemetry
seg-76679b4d7efd = <c1/>: Render a GNSS trust stream or a `receiver-trust` result as security telemetry text (`kshana trust-telemetry`, docs/TRUST-TELEMETRY.md), with the same encoders the command line uses: `prometheus` (the exposition of the final state), `cef` or `leef` (one RFC { "5424" } syslog line per band change, or per epoch with `syslog_all`; no timestamp, because no clock is read) or `otlp` (the OTLP/HTTP JSON metrics payload).

# text
# c1: validate_scenario
seg-e20044725e3a = <c1/>: Pre-flight check of a Kshana scenario TOML: verify it parses as TOML and detect its scenario kind, without running it.

# text
# c1: verify_evidence_pack
seg-2598d06895ec = <c1/>: Verify a signed evidence pack (`kshana evidence verify`): every file's SHA-256 against the manifest, the hash chain, the Ed25519 signature, and with `public_key` (the signer's key, from the signer by another route) that the signer is the one expected, and with `full_log` that the log you hold is the one the pack records and that the slice came from it.

# text
seg-62b5564e8d90 = The recorded session plays here step by step.

# text
# c1: clock-holdover.toml
seg-2359edf78388 = <b>Same run in the Studio:</b> Engine v{ $version } · <c1/> · seed { "42" }

